We re-examine the lepton flavour universal violation in $b 	o l l$ flavour transitions, particularly focusing on the anomaly in the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ and $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ decays of the bottom quark. By analysing the anomalous lepton flavour universality in these decays and assuming Standard Model effective field theory (SMEFT) new physics (NP) operators, we place constraints on the dimension-6 effective coefficients based on an extensive range of experimental data. Our study reveals a significant correlation between the anomalous NP couplings in the $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ and $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions. Moreover, our work demonstrates the significance of updating the analysis of the $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ decay rate provided by the LHCb in recent years. Based on the obtained bounds, we also infer a set of NP scenarios in the quark-lepton flavour mixing matrices that could be experimentally tested at the LHC.
